g if you do that  i recommend using a lower bar position  because obviously when you're leaning  forwards you don't want the weight to be  rolling towards your neck at the same  time of course the bar needs to stay  over the top of the center of your feet  the entire way through you don't want it  in front of you because then you're at a  very bad angle and if you don't face  plant then you might find that your back  gives out so  this is just quite a technical lift if  you're going to add weight then make  sure you know what you're doing if  you're not adding weights then you can  still imagine that bar being over the  center of your toes in order to make  sure you're using the correct form  this is another one i learned from  watching knees over toes guy ben patrick  i'm just going to level with you he's  got so much fantastic information i  could just give you a whole bunch of his  exercises and they'd all be fantastic  for bulletproofing and improving  performance as well this one was  particularly useful for me however and  that's why i integrated it into my own  training that's the tibialis anterior  raise so basically what you're going to  be doing is raising your toes upwards  using the tibialis anterior and the  tibialis anterior is the muscle on your  shin on the front and it's responsible  for dorsiflexion lifting your foot  upwards now you might think this is a  kind of pointless muscle because i mean  you're always pushing through the ground  with your foot when you're running or  jumping but how often do you need to  lift your foot upwards with any kind of  force well one obvious example of how  you might train this naturally if we  were in a more varied environment is  through swimming however it's also  important because we use it to absorb a  lot of impact when we land from a jump  this is the kind of first line of  defense when you're landing from a jump  when your legs are impacting on the  ground as you're running and this is one  of the things that causes shin splints  [Music]  so to do this movement all you're going  to do i'm leaning against the wall here  is lean against the wall  and then you're just going to raise your  toes upwards  as high as you can  then you can lean the angle back  slightly  and the great thing about this  is that your legs are going to be in the  position that they would be if you are  decelerating or often landing from a  jump  where your shins are coming down at an  angle  and another trick you can actually use  is just to walk on your heels with your  toes constantly pointing upwards it  doesn't take long for this to create a  surprising burn in the front of the legs  there another thing you can do to try  and increase the difficulty slightly is  to use a wedge and to put your heels  slightly higher than your toes and this  allows you to travel further and  increases the difficulty i also  recommend you flip this round and do it  the other way so that you're performing  a calf stretch and then a calf raise  next up is one of my favorite exercises  which i've talked about a lot on this  channel before i did a whole video on  them and that's the inverted push-up  also known as the bodyweight row various  other names basically what you're doing  here is you're hanging underneath the  bar or parallel bars with your hands  you're extending fully and then you're  pulling yourself up so that your sternum  reaches the bar or comes close to where  your hands are located and what you're  doing here is not only obviously  training the lats and the shoulder to a  lesser extent but you're also  maintaining a rigid position with your  core once again and specifically this is  an anti-flexion movement what that means  is that your body wants to bend this way  because your heels are on the floor your  butt wants to sink towards the ground  which means you need to extend which is  anti-flexion so you're once again  bracing the erector spinae your whole  back nice and straight and just  maintaining this kind of rigidity this  is really important because we train the  opposite so often when we're doing  push-ups if you do plank and you're  training it specifically and we're  always in a hunched over forwards  position when we're sitting at a desk so  in other words this is what you need to  balance yourself out and if you do a lot  of push-ups it's a really good idea to  also do lots of bodyweight rows an added  bonus if you also make sure that you're  fully retracting the shoulder blades  when you're at the top of the movement  then this is also going to train your  straight arm strength and your scapular  control and this is really useful when  it comes to things like rose because it  allows you to support the shoulder more  so and avoid shoulder injury as well  another move from another culture that  you might not have used in the past is  the sumo squat with shikoh not like  chico time this is the leg lift that you  sometimes see sumo wrestlers do where  they raise up one side and then slam the  leg down the sumo squat is really good  because it activates the hip adductors  it creates a nice stretch and it trains  your legs and your hips in the frontal  plane we still often move forwards and  backwards never side to side and this  can lead to injury whether you're  running on an uneven surface or you're  suddenly changing direction if you don't  have that strength there this can lead  to issues and it helps to keep the knee  tracking nicely where it's supposed to  be stacking the joints then by adding in  that leg raise as well you're raising  your leg up and so you're strengthening  it in both directions you're also  building stability and balance on one  leg which of course is really useful  whatever kind of sport you're engaging  in so i highly recommend trying this out  and now there is a proper way to perform  chico i'd probably completely butcher it  but you don't necessarily have to use  the exact same technique the idea is  that you're squatting down nice and low  and then you're raising yourself up and  raising that leg out to the side before  moving back onto the other side shift  your leg onto the supporting side as for  the sumo squat itself you just want to  take your feet slightly wider than  shoulder width apart slightly wider than  you normally would turn your toes out  slightly about 45 degrees and then turn  the knees out as well make sure that  they're following the feet you don't  want them turning inwards that kind of  defeats the point and that can cause  pain in and of itself and obviously this  builds that sweet hip mobility as well  next one i've been using this is one  that's particularly useful for me  personally may not apply to you is the  camel pose and here what you're doing is  you're getting onto your knees and then  you're trying to extend the back  focusing on the thoracic spine you do  this by pushing the sternum up towards  the sky and opening up the chest and  this will help to improve not only  mobility in your spine but also  proprioception so that you can actually  feel and control the spine you can also  do edo portals favorite the spinal wave  if you want to do this properly you need  to sit up on your knees and then reach  all the way back and proper yogis can  grab their ankles and do all sorts of  cool stuff i'm nowhere near that but i  basically just want to be able to  control my back so that i bend more of  the thoracic spine and less at the  lumbar spine because that's another way  that mobility can really enhance  longevity and prevent injury because  we'd much rather that thoracic spine was  bending rather than the lumbar spine and  if you don't have that mobility then  it's going to have to go somewhere else  basically if you look at a back bridge  the best ones bend all the way around  and particularly at the middle of the  spine not so much down the bottom if  you're just bending at the bottom it  probably means that you don't have  enough spine mobility also probably not  enough shoulder mobility so you want to  work on both those things someone like  dr stuart mcgill would tell you not to  extend the spine in this way because it  causes too much compression personally i  want to be able to do things like  hamstrings so it's not an issue i do  believe that you can do this safely and  into old age however like i said your  mileage may vary it depends on what you  want from your training if you're just  doing big heavy lifts you don't  necessarily need or want this kind of  mobility  [Music]  as for the shoulders one of the best  things you can do to improve mobility  there and thereby prevent that kind of  cascading effect is hangs which also  builds a lot of grip strength stretches  out the lats and at the same time they  create traction on the spine moving it  nicely apart and just helping it to feel  healthy and loose they do say after all  that you are as old as your spine tang's  also fantastic because they're an  isometric hold and isometrics are a  brilliant way to train anything when it  comes to recovery because you're not  moving through a range of motion thereby  you're minimizing the risk of injury but  you're also sending the blood to that  area and just like high flush sets this  can also work as a form of occlusion  basically trapping the blood in the  muscle building up metabolites and  really accelerating that recovery  if you want to perform the hand  correctly with the focus being on  shoulder mobility and stretching out the  lats then you want to perform it  passively what that means is that you  want to only contract intense muscles  that need to be contracted and tense so  you want to relax the rest of the body  and particularly the lats you might  think you're hanging completely without  inserting any strength but then you'll  notice that you can actually relax more  and shrink down into it further bringing  your shoulders up more and feeling that  stretch in your lats and that's what we  want if the focus is mobility which for  me it is  so this next one i like because we can  do it sitting down i'm talking about  wrist curls both supinated wrist curls  and pronated wrist curls and these are  important not only to strengthen the  wrists but also to prevent injury in the  elbows and that's because a lot of the  tendons in the forearm they span both  the wrist joint and the elbow joint for  example you have the carpi radialis  brevis and this goes from the third  metacarpal through to the lateral  epicondyle at the end of the humerus and  so by strengthening the wrist you can  prevent things like tennis elbow or  lateral epicondylitis  so you want to use a weight that's light  enough that you can wrist curl support  your forearm on something and then  you're moving literally just the wrist  and the important thing here is  obviously balance because we do so much  wrist flexion that we also need wrist  extension otherwise we're going to  become lopsided and this is what leads  to injuries now my video talking about  adapting to your environment i said how  it could actually be a reductionist  approach try and chase down every single  last muscle group that gets overlooked  and to try and create balance that way  this is certainly true the best way to  create balance in your grip is just to  use your arms and hands in a lot of  different ways the best way to create  hip stability and knee stability is to  walk a lot on uneven terrain the best  way to balance out dorsiflexion and  plantar flexion is to do some swimming  as well let the environment shape you  because it has more variables to create  a more well-rounded physique however  this isn't always possible especially if  you don't have space to go running  through the woods and so this is where  looking at particular weak areas that go  wrong and making sure to add in some  functional training there can be really  useful and this is why i take issue with  people who talk about you know strength  being strength as long as you're doing  your big three lifts you don't need  anything else because it doesn't work  like that you don't build strong squats  and then suddenly you have perfect  balance in your forearms and this kind  of focus on just one type of strength is  what leads to imbalances and then later  pain and that's why i think it's really  important to make sure you're training  in balance and not just focusing on one  particular skill follow the link in the  description down below it will take you  to the bioneer blog where you'll find  not only a full routine using these  exercises with sets and reps and things  but also a few additional exercises that  i also use as part of my fortify workout  that's the point here as well these are  exercises that benefit me that i do on a  regular basis because they train the  things that i'm looking for they fix  weak areas and they support the kind of  movements i want you might not  necessarily benefit from these same  exercises and that's why we need to look  around and we need to find different  things and adapt them to our own use we  need to absorb what is useful reject  what is not and add what is uniquely our  own  i just came up with that no idea you  know just popped into my head anyways i  hope that you can find some exercises in  this routine that are useful and add  them to your own training and get some  benefits as a result thank you so much  for watching this one guys if you found  it useful interesting then please leave  a like and share around that helps me  out immensely if you like this kind of  training that looks at functional  performance and trying to build balance  and longevity then you'll enjoy my ebook  and training program super functional  training there's a discount on right now  and there's a link in the description  down below where you can check it out i  also have a print book called functional  training and beyond which is an  introduction to the concept of  functional training as it applies to  athletes and the average joe either way  thank you so much for watching and i'll  see you next time  bye for now
 

